1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,365
Hello!

2
00:00:00,365 --> 00:00:04,015
Today we learn phrases for a restaurant in Gamcheon Village.

3
00:00:04,015 --> 00:00:06,692
Not sure what to say at a Korean restaurant?

4
00:00:06,692 --> 00:00:09,673
Learn these one by one and order with confidence.

5
00:00:09,673 --> 00:00:10,220
Let's go!

6
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:20,379
Walking around, we get hungry. We say '배고파요'.

7
00:00:20,379 --> 00:00:24,900
'배' is your stomach, '고파요' means you need food.

8
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,690
Let's find a good restaurant.

9
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,006
A restaurant has a flow: find, order, taste, pay.

10
00:00:37,006 --> 00:00:39,660
Today we learn each step with the right phrase.

11
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:52,001
In a new area,

12
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:55,153
ask a passerby: '이 근처에 맛있는 식당이 어디 있어요'?

13
00:00:55,153 --> 00:00:57,577
(Where's a good restaurant? ).

14
00:00:57,577 --> 00:00:59,920
This line gets you kind help.

15
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:07,280
Hard to choose? Ask '추천할 만한 식당 있어요'?.

16
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,240
'추천' means suggesting something good.

17
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:18,175
For local specialties, ask '여기 뭐가 유명해요'?.

18
00:01:18,175 --> 00:01:21,550
'유명해요' means well-loved by many.

19
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:30,078
Ask '이 집은 어떤 걸 팔아요'? (What do you sell? ).

20
00:01:30,078 --> 00:01:34,580
'팔아요' means to sell. Handy before going in.

21
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:43,108
When it looks tasty, say '맛있어 보여요'.

22
00:01:43,108 --> 00:01:46,841
'-어 보여요' means it seems so by looking.

23
00:01:46,841 --> 00:01:48,020
Let's go in!

24
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,078
The staff asks '몇 분이세요'?

25
00:01:55,078 --> 00:01:57,268
— meaning 'how many people'?

26
00:01:57,268 --> 00:01:59,850
They're checking your group size.

27
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:10,062
Alone? '한 명이요'. Two? '두 명이요'. '명' counts people.

28
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:13,640
Show fingers too for clarity.

29
00:02:18,270 --> 00:02:23,055
See a seat? Ask '여기 앉아도 돼요'? (May I sit here? ).

30
00:02:23,055 --> 00:02:28,040
'-아도 돼요'? asks permission. When they say yes, sit.

31
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:36,301
Seated, you need the menu: '메뉴 주세요'.

32
00:02:36,301 --> 00:02:41,240
'메뉴' is the list, '주세요' is a polite request.

33
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:48,183
Not sure what to pick? Ask '여기 뭐가 맛있어요'?.

34
00:02:48,183 --> 00:02:51,310
They'll tell you what they do best.

35
00:02:54,570 --> 00:02:57,817
Ask '인기 메뉴가 뭐예요'? (What's popular? ).

36
00:02:57,817 --> 00:03:01,240
The popular dish is usually a safe bet.

37
00:03:04,210 --> 00:03:08,260
Ask the staff '메뉴를 추천해 주세요' (Please recommend).

38
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:11,190
It means 'pick a good one for me'.

39
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:20,850
Point at a new dish: '이건 뭐예요'? (What is this? ).

40
00:03:20,850 --> 00:03:23,710
'이건' is 'this'. Just ask!

41
00:03:30,570 --> 00:03:33,810
Ask '무엇으로 만들었어요'? (What's it made of? ).

42
00:03:33,810 --> 00:03:37,050
Always check if you can't eat something.

43
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:46,558
Common ingredients: '쌀' (rice), '고기' (meat),

44
00:03:46,558 --> 00:03:51,161
'채소' (vegetables), '해물' (seafood).

45
00:03:51,161 --> 00:03:54,140
These four cover most.

46
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:59,216
Ask '어떤 맛이에요'? (How does it taste? ).

47
00:03:59,216 --> 00:04:01,470
Knowing helps you choose.

48
00:04:04,970 --> 00:04:09,212
Not into spicy? Ask '이거 매워요'? (Is it spicy? ).

49
00:04:09,212 --> 00:04:13,270
Korean food is often spicy — a key question.

50
00:04:14,010 --> 00:04:18,365
Ask '안 매운 거 있어요'? (A non-spicy one? ).

51
00:04:18,365 --> 00:04:22,720
'안' means 'not' — something not spicy.

52
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:33,910
Sharing or hungry? Ask '양이 많아요'? (Big portion? ).

53
00:04:33,910 --> 00:04:36,180
'양' is the amount.

54
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:41,850
Chose? Point and say '이거 주세요' (This, please).

55
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:44,450
The most-used ordering phrase.

56
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:56,289
Say the count: '이거 하나하고 저거 둘 주세요'.

57
00:04:56,289 --> 00:05:00,440
Near is '이거,' far is '저거'.

58
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:09,229
Thirsty? '물 좀 주세요' (Water, please). Water is free.

59
00:05:09,229 --> 00:05:11,540
'좀' makes it softer.

60
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:16,155
Food's here! Before eating,

61
00:05:16,155 --> 00:05:20,150
say '잘 먹겠습니다' — a thank-you to whoever made it.

62
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:30,574
Out of side dishes? '반찬 좀 더 주세요'. '더' means more.

63
00:05:30,574 --> 00:05:33,290
Refills are usually free.

64
00:05:35,830 --> 00:05:40,560
No utensils? '젓가락하고 숟가락 주세요'.

65
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:45,290
'젓가락' picks up, '숟가락' scoops.

66
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:52,150
Want more? '이거 리필 돼요'? (Refill? ).

67
00:05:52,150 --> 00:05:55,090
'리필' means refilling.

68
00:05:56,070 --> 00:05:59,073
Describe the taste. Good? '맛있어요'!

69
00:05:59,073 --> 00:06:03,440
A thumbs-up helps. A big compliment to the cook.

70
00:06:07,730 --> 00:06:13,161
Spicy is '매워요,' salty is '짜요'.

71
00:06:13,161 --> 00:06:18,410
Add '조금' to soften: '조금 매워요'.

72
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:24,037
Sweet is '달아요,' bland is '싱거워요'.

73
00:06:24,037 --> 00:06:27,020
Each taste has its word.

74
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:33,614
Clean is '담백해요,' nutty is '고소해요'.

75
00:06:33,614 --> 00:06:37,240
Perfect to praise the broth.

76
00:06:39,770 --> 00:06:46,414
Let's review the taste words: spicy '맵다',

77
00:06:46,414 --> 00:06:54,031
salty '짜다', sweet '달다', sour '시다', bitter '쓰다',

78
00:06:54,031 --> 00:07:01,000
light '담백하다', savory '고소하다', greasy '느끼하다'.

79
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,510
Use the right one for each flavor!

80
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,240
Done? '잘 먹었습니다'.

81
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:15,081
Before eating '잘 먹겠습니다,' after '잘 먹었습니다'.

82
00:07:15,081 --> 00:07:18,500
Remember them as a pair.

83
00:07:20,510 --> 00:07:25,729
Leftovers? '남은 음식을 싸 주시겠어요'? (Pack it? ).

84
00:07:25,729 --> 00:07:28,530
'싸다' means to wrap up.

85
00:07:29,650 --> 00:07:34,004
Go to the counter: '계산할게요, 얼마예요'?

86
00:07:34,004 --> 00:07:39,150
'계산' is paying, '얼마예요'? asks the price.

87
00:07:42,870 --> 00:07:46,783
Card: '카드 돼요'? Cash: '현금으로 할게요'.

88
00:07:46,783 --> 00:07:50,330
Small shops may be cash-only.

89
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:58,300
Need a receipt? '영수증 주세요'. It shows what you paid.

90
00:08:00,690 --> 00:08:04,224
Loved it? '여기 진짜 맛있었어요, 또 오고 싶어요'!

91
00:08:04,224 --> 00:08:07,550
A warm word makes a nice memory.

92
00:08:09,680 --> 00:08:12,101
Today's flow: find, order, taste, pay.

93
00:08:12,101 --> 00:08:14,840
You know the phrases now, right? Great job!

94
00:08:23,910 --> 00:08:26,510
Use today's phrases at a restaurant.

95
00:08:26,510 --> 00:08:29,398
The more you use them, the more natural.

96
00:08:29,398 --> 00:08:31,420
Next: cafe expressions. Bye!

